Subject: [PATCH] onboarding: allow rejected providers to reset and resubmit
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phase 1 micro-checkpoint 1 — server state transitions only.

Provider owners can now recover from a rejection without help. The
application shows the reviewer's rejection reason, the owner explicitly
resets it back to draft, edits their answers, and resubmits — the same
short loop they'd expect from any careful review process. Every previous
rejection stays visible in a read-only history so both the provider and
future reviewers keep the context they need.

- POST /providers/application/reset (rejected → draft), idempotent on draft,
  409 on under_review/approved/suspended; snapshots the closed cycle into
  the new provider_application_submissions history table under a
  SELECT … FOR UPDATE serialization.
- provider_applications gains a provider-visible rejection_reason column;
  reviewer_notes remains admin-private and never leaves the server for
  application responses or history payloads.
- PATCH /providers/application and POST /providers/application/submit now
  require an explicit reset before touching a rejected application.
- GET /providers/application returns rejectionReason and a public
  previousSubmissions[] history alongside the existing state.
- Approved-provider authorization gate unchanged; draft/rejected/
  under_review still cannot use provider operations.
- Focused test:provider-resubmission covers ownership, idempotency,
  concurrency (5 concurrent resets → exactly 1 history row), rejected→
  draft→under_review, multi-cycle history, and approved-provider
  regression (11/11 passing).
- Regression suites green locally: authorization 7/7, care-history 4/4,
  role-state 2/2, reviews 7/7, availability 3/3, booking state-machine
  63/63, booking-concurrency 16/16, booking-pressure 13/13, full workspace
  typecheck and build.
